WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.430
I actually read a story about a woman who

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:04.270
faked a pregnancy to fool her fiance.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.730
And I started to dig into how she did it,

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:09.230
and I found out there was actually websites,

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:10.550
fake pregnancy websites.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.670
And then I always start asking, "What if?"

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.070
I know the beginning, I know the end.

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:20.470
I always have to know that twist ending before we start.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:25.390
If you've seen The Stranger, I knew what happened,

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:27.950
when she vanished in the very beginning of that story.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.550
I need to make sure I nail episode one and eight,

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:38.630
and then the harder ones actually are the ones in the middle.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.150
I don't like any kind of structure,

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:42.990
at least consciously thinking about it.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:46.430
E.L. Doctorow had one of my favorite quotes on writing where

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.610
he said that, "Writing is like driving at night in the fog

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:50.710
with just your headlights on."

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:54.670
I just need you to tell me the truth.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:58.670
The key to any protagonist is they have to be real.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.390
There has to be that character that when you're sitting at a bar, 

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:04.190
you kind of want to overhear the conversation a little bit.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.450
The best twists come really from the heart.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.670
You could have the most expensive car in the world,

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.110
but if it doesn't have fuel,

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:13.370
it's not going any place.

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:16.030
And this is true of all fiction we've ever done,

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:19.030
is your hero travels down the road of life,

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:23.590
and you've got to put speed bumps and pitfalls and detours.

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:26.030
It's really hard to figure out how to navigate all the hurdles

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:27.170
and the twists and turns.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:29.230
And when you're giving information,

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:31.390
when you're giving too much or too little information,

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:33.510
it's a balancing act.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:36.950
It's kind of always fun to sort of see where it's doing well.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:40.510
I'll be sitting there going, "Oh my God, this is terrible

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:41.950
I messed it all up.

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:44.690
I was so good before, what happened to me?

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:45.530
How'd I lose it?"

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:49.510
And then five minutes later, you'll go, "This is genius."

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:58.410
What I'm trying to write is a story that will be universal

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.750
and I've always found the more specific, the locale, the

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:03.090
the more universal the appeal.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:08.190
The themes are universal, love, redemption, family,

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.949
friendship, relationships, hope.

00:02:10.949 --> 00:02:13.190
Those things are universal.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:17.110
When I'm writing a book like The Stranger or doing the TV show

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:18.870
I become completely obsessed.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.430
I think about it night and day, all of the time,

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:24.070
I wake up thinking about it.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.670
I'm a distracted friend or guest, you know,

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:28.350
if you're talking to me, my kids are used to it,

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:31.110
like all of a sudden, I'll start giving one of these.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.870
And the kids'll go, "Oh, Dad's gone off to la la land."

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.970
You have to learn by making those mistakes,

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:37.400
someone else can't make them for you.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:38.110
Thanks.

00:02:38.326 --> 00:02:38.990
Take care, guys.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:39.950
Bye.

